export const PS6={title:'Filming: camera, light and composition',hook:'Judges score exposure, focus and color and camera technique and composition at 10 points each—20 points that depend on how you film. You do not need expensive equipment; a modern phone can produce broadcast-quality video if you control light, stability, framing and focus. This lesson covers the filming skills that make a PSA look professional.',objectives:['Control exposure, focus and color when filming.','Use shot types, angles and movement purposefully.','Apply composition principles such as the rule of thirds.','Plan a smooth, efficient filming day.'],sections:[
['Exposure, focus and color',[
'**Exposure** is how bright or dark an image is. Underexposed shots look dark and grainy; overexposed shots lose detail in bright areas. On a phone, tap to set **focus and exposure** on your subject, and lock them if possible so they do not change mid-shot.',
'**Focus** should be sharp on the subject’s eyes or the key action. **Color** should look natural and consistent between shots—avoid mixing very different light sources (such as warm indoor bulbs and cool daylight) in the same scene, or set the white balance.',
'The top score requires sharp, in-focus images and highly effective lighting that tells the story.',
"Clean the camera lens before every shoot. Fingerprints and smudges on phone lenses are a common cause of hazy, unfocused footage that is easy to prevent.",
sort('Good or poor image quality?',['Good','Poor'],[['Subject’s face sharp and evenly lit',0],['Background bright, face in shadow',1],['Exposure locked so brightness stays steady',0],['Shot keeps refocusing while filming',1],['Consistent color between shots',0]])]],
['Lighting',[
'**Natural light** from a window is soft and flattering—place the subject facing the window, not with the window behind them. Outdoors, overcast days give even light; in bright sun, use shade or film in the morning or late afternoon.',
'**Three-point lighting** uses a **key light** (main light), a **fill light** (softens shadows) and a **back light** (separates the subject from the background). Simple lamps or reflectors (white poster board) can work. Avoid harsh overhead lights that create shadows under eyes.',
'Lighting can also set mood: bright, warm light suits a hopeful message.',
"Film all shots in a scene under the same lighting conditions when possible. If daylight changes during filming, shots edited together may not match in brightness or color.",
match('Lighting term and role',[['Key light','Main light on the subject'],['Fill light','Softens shadows'],['Back light','Separates subject from background'],['Reflector','Bounces light to fill shadows'],['Backlighting by a window','Makes the subject a silhouette'],['Soft light','Flattering, even illumination']])]],
['Shot types and angles',[
'Use a variety of **shot types**: **wide (establishing)** shots show the setting; **medium** shots show a person from the waist up; **close-ups** show emotion or detail; **extreme close-ups** emphasize small details; and **over-the-shoulder** shots show conversations.',
'**Camera angles** shape meaning: **eye level** feels natural, **low angle** makes a subject look powerful, **high angle** can make a subject look small and a **point-of-view** shot shows what a character sees. Short PSAs benefit from quick, purposeful variety.',
"Plan each shot’s purpose before filming. Ask, “What should viewers notice in this shot?” and choose the shot type and angle that make that detail clear.",
match('Shot or angle and effect',[['Wide shot','Shows the setting'],['Medium shot','Shows a person and gestures'],['Close-up','Shows emotion'],['Low angle','Makes a subject look powerful'],['High angle','Makes a subject look small'],['Point-of-view shot','Shows what a character sees']])]],
['Composition',[
'The **rule of thirds** divides the frame into a three-by-three grid; placing subjects or eyes along these lines or at intersections creates balanced, interesting shots. Leave **headroom** (space above the head) but not too much, and **lead room** (space in the direction a person faces or moves).',
'Keep backgrounds **uncluttered** and relevant, and check for distracting objects. Hold the camera **horizontally** (landscape) unless your platform requires vertical video, and keep the horizon level.',
'Composition and camera technique together earn up to 10 points.',
"If your PSA will be shown mainly on vertical platforms, plan composition for that format from the start, keeping important action and text in the center of the frame.",
sort('Good composition?',['Good composition','Poor composition'],[['Eyes on the upper third line',0],['A pole appearing to grow from someone’s head',1],['Lead room in the direction of movement',0],['Tilted horizon by accident',1],['Clean, relevant background',0]])]],
['Stability and movement',[
'Shaky footage distracts viewers. Use a **tripod**, a **stabilizer** or brace the phone against your body or a steady surface. Move the camera **purposefully**: smooth **pans** (side to side), **tilts** (up and down), **tracking** shots (following a subject) and **push-ins** can add energy.',
'The rubric warns against scenes with too much movement that distract from the message. Hold each shot steady for a few seconds before and after the action to give editors room.',
"Keep camera movement motivated by the story. A smooth push-in as a character has a realization feels purposeful, while random movement distracts from the message.",
chk('Why hold a shot steady before and after the action?',['To give the editor clean footage for transitions','To make the PSA longer','To save battery','It is not useful'],'Editing room.')]],
['Planning the filming day',[
'Follow your **shot list** grouped by location. Bring charged batteries, extra storage, a tripod, lights or reflectors, a microphone, props, costumes, printed scripts and signed releases. Assign roles: **director**, **camera operator**, **sound recorder**, **continuity checker** and **actors**.',
'Film **multiple takes** of each shot and a few extra shots (**B-roll**) of details and settings to use during editing. Check footage on the spot for focus, exposure and sound.',
"Keep a continuity log of costumes, props and positions. In a short PSA, a cup that switches hands between shots is easy for judges to notice.",
ex('Filming checklist','Prepare for a two-hour filming session (items invented for practice).',['Equipment: phone, tripod, lavalier mic, reflector, power bank','Paperwork: shot list, script, actor releases, location permission','Roles: director, camera, sound, continuity','Plan: 3 takes per shot plus 5 minutes of B-roll per location'],'A prepared crew films efficiently and avoids reshoots (details invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('On set',{a:{text:'Your actor stands in front of a bright window and looks dark on screen. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Turn the actor to face the window so the light falls on their face','b','Correct.',true],['Keep filming—it is fine','b','Backlighting creates a silhouette.',false]]},b:{text:'Handheld footage is shaky. What is the best fix?',choices:[['Use a tripod or brace the camera and move smoothly','c','Correct.',true],['Shake it more for style','c','Too much movement distracts.',false]]},c:{text:'Your footage is sharp, well lit and steady.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Control exposure, focus and color: tap and lock focus and exposure, keep the subject sharp and use consistent light and white balance (exposure/focus/color is worth 10 points).','Use soft natural light facing the subject or three-point lighting (key, fill, back); vary shot types (wide, medium, close-up) and angles (eye level, low, high, point of view) purposefully.','Compose with the rule of thirds, headroom, lead room and clean backgrounds; stabilize with a tripod and move smoothly; follow the shot list, film multiple takes and B-roll and check footage on set.'],terms:[['Exposure','How bright or dark an image appears.'],['Rule of thirds','Placing subjects along a three-by-three grid for balanced framing.'],['B-roll','Supplementary footage used to support the main shots.']],mcqs:[q('How should an actor face a window for good lighting?','Facing the window','With the window behind them','Sideways in darkness','It does not matter','Lighting.'),q('Which light is the main light on the subject?','Key light','Fill light','Back light','Practical light','Three-point lighting.'),q('Which shot shows emotion best?','Close-up','Wide shot','Establishing shot','Aerial shot','Shot types.'),q('A low angle makes a subject look:','Powerful','Small','Distant','Blurry','Angles.'),q('The rule of thirds places subjects:','Along grid lines or intersections','Always in the exact center','At the very edge','Upside down','Composition.'),q('What reduces shaky footage?','A tripod or stabilizer','Running while filming','Zooming constantly','Holding the phone loosely','Stability.'),q('What is B-roll?','Supplementary footage','A type of microphone','The final credits','A copyright form','Editing.'),q('How many technical quality items are worth 10 points each?','Four','One','Two','Six','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would film the PSA to earn high scores for exposure, focus, color and camera technique.',rubric:[['Exposure and focus',['exposure','focus','lock','sharp','color','white balance']],['Lighting',['natural light','window','key','fill','back','reflector']],['Shots and composition',['wide','medium','close-up','angle','rule of thirds','headroom','background']],['Stability and planning',['tripod','smooth','takes','b-roll','shot list','check footage']]],model:'We would film with a modern phone, tapping to set focus and exposure on each actor’s face and locking them so brightness and focus stay steady, and we would avoid mixing warm and cool light so color stays consistent. Indoors, we would position actors facing a window for soft, natural light, using a white poster board as a reflector to fill shadows, and for night scenes we would set up simple three-point lighting with a lamp as the key light, a softer fill light and a small back light to separate the actor from the background. We would vary shots purposefully: a wide shot to establish the kitchen, a medium shot of friends dancing, a close-up of a student smiling after a deep breath and an extreme close-up of a hand lowering headphone volume, with a low angle to make our “brain coach” character look confident. We would compose with the rule of thirds, placing eyes on the upper third line, leaving lead room as actors move, keeping backgrounds clean and level and filming horizontally. To keep footage steady, we would use a tripod and make only smooth pans and tracking shots, holding each shot a few seconds before and after the action for editing. On filming day, we would follow our shot list by location, assign a director, camera operator, sound recorder and continuity checker, bring charged batteries, a microphone and signed releases, film three takes of each shot plus B-roll and check every clip on the spot for focus, exposure and sound.'},sources:[RULES,COMM]};

export const PS7={title:'Audio and editing',hook:'Viewers forgive slightly imperfect video far more readily than bad sound. Audio and editing are each worth 10 points: judges look for a balanced approach to sound, appropriate use of silence and music, clean transitions and tight synchronization. Good editing turns raw footage into a polished 30-second story.',objectives:['Record clear dialogue and narration.','Balance voice, music and sound effects.','Edit with clean transitions and synchronization.','Finish a show-ready PSA within 30 seconds.'],sections:[
['Recording clear audio',[
'Record in a **quiet** space, away from fans, traffic and echoing rooms. Place the microphone **close** to the speaker—a **lavalier (clip-on) mic** or a phone placed just out of frame often sounds better than the camera’s built-in mic across the room. Soft furnishings such as curtains and pillows reduce echo.',
'Record a few seconds of **room tone** (silence in the location) to help smooth edits. Wear headphones to monitor sound while recording, and re-record lines with noise problems.',
'Voice-over narration can be recorded separately in a quiet closet lined with clothes.',
"Turn off notifications and set phones to airplane mode during recording to prevent interruptions and interference with audio.",
"Record a second backup audio source when possible, such as a phone recording near the speaker. If one recording has a problem, the other can save the scene.",
sort('Helps or hurts audio quality?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Clip-on microphone near the speaker',0],['Recording next to a loud air conditioner',1],['Recording a voice-over in a closet with clothes',0],['Camera mic across a large gym',1],['Monitoring with headphones',0]])]],
['Balancing sound',[
'The top audio score requires an **effective and balanced approach**: speech is always clear, **music sits beneath dialogue** and **silence** is used intentionally for impact. Lower music volume (“ducking”) when someone speaks, and raise it during visual-only moments.',
'Avoid levels that are too loud (distorted) or too soft. Check the final mix on phone speakers and headphones, since judges may watch either way.',
'Sound effects can add humor and energy when used sparingly.',
"Use music with a mood that matches each part of the PSA. An upbeat track supports a playful montage, while a softer moment might need gentle music or silence.",
"Listen for distracting noises such as hums, clicks or wind in the final mix. Many editing apps include simple noise-reduction tools, but it is better to avoid noise while recording.",
match('Audio element and best use',[['Dialogue or narration','Always clear and understandable'],['Background music','Beneath dialogue; sets mood'],['Silence','A pause for emphasis'],['Sound effect','A quick “ding” as a habit is completed'],['Ducking','Lowering music under speech'],['Room tone','Smooths cuts between clips']])]],
['Editing basics',[
'Editing software ranges from free phone apps to desktop programs. Basic steps: **import** footage, **select** the best takes, **arrange** clips on a timeline following the storyboard, **trim** each clip tightly, add **transitions**, **titles and captions**, **music and sound effects**, **color correction** and **export**.',
'Team members must do all editing themselves, though they may receive instruction from an outside source. Keep a project backup in case files are lost.',
"Watch the rough cut with fresh eyes the next day, or ask classmates who have not seen it. They will notice confusing moments or slow sections that the team overlooked.",
order('Editing workflow',['Import and organize footage','Select the best takes','Arrange clips on the timeline','Trim clips tightly','Add transitions, titles and captions','Add and balance music and sound','Color correct and export'])]],
['Transitions and pacing',[
'Most professional edits use simple **cuts**; fancy transitions can look amateur. Use **match cuts** (cutting between similar shapes or motions), **J-cuts and L-cuts** (audio leads or trails the video) and occasional **fades** for smooth flow.',
'Pacing should match the tone: quick cuts for an energetic montage, longer shots for an emotional moment. In 30 seconds, most shots last **1–3 seconds**.',
"Remove any moment that does not support the message. In 30 seconds, even a one-second pause that serves no purpose can be cut to make room for clearer content.",
"Test pacing by watching the PSA without sound. If the story still makes sense and the call to action is clear, the visual editing is doing its job.",
chk('Which transition is most common in professional editing?',['A simple cut','A spinning cube','A star wipe','A page curl'],'Clean cuts look professional.')]],
['Synchronization',[
'**Synchronization** means sound and video happen together: lips match words, sound effects match actions and music beats can match cuts. Out-of-sync audio is distracting and loses points.',
'When recording audio separately, **clap** on camera at the start of each take; aligning the clap’s sound spike with the moment hands meet syncs the clip. Cutting on music beats gives a polished, rhythmic feel.',
"Use consistent color correction so every clip looks like part of the same video. Small adjustments to brightness and warmth across clips create a polished, professional feel.",
ex('Syncing separately recorded audio','Sync a clip recorded with a separate microphone.',['At the start of the take, a teammate claps once on camera','In the editor, find the frame where the hands meet','Find the sharp spike in the separate audio track','Align the spike with that frame and check lip sync'],'Audio and video now match precisely.')]],
['Titles, captions and the time limit',[
'Titles, credits and captions are part of the 30 seconds. Keep titles brief, use readable fonts and add **captions** for accessibility. Check the final runtime from the **first fade, visual or sound to the last**; optional **pure black** leads at the beginning and end do not count.',
'Export at high quality (for example, 1080p) in a common format, watch the final file on several devices and confirm the runtime before uploading.',
"Save several versions as you edit, labeled with dates. If a change does not work, you can return to an earlier version instead of starting over.",
sort('Counts toward the 30 seconds?',['Counts','Does not count'],[['Opening title card',0],['End credits',0],['Optional pure black lead at the start',1],['Captions on screen during dialogue',0],['Optional pure black lead at the end',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Editing review',{a:{text:'In your rough cut, background music drowns out the narration. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Lower (duck) the music under the narration','b','Correct.',true],['Raise the music for energy','b','Speech must be clear.',false]]},b:{text:'Your final export runs 31 seconds, with 1 second of pure black at the end. Is it within the limit?',choices:[['Yes, if the content from first visual or sound to last is 30 seconds or less','c','Correct—pure black leads do not count.',true],['No, any black counts','c','Only content between the first and last visual or sound counts.',false]]},c:{text:'Your PSA is balanced, synchronized and within time.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Record in quiet spaces with the mic close (lavalier or nearby phone), capture room tone and monitor with headphones; audio is worth 10 points.','Balance sound: clear speech, music ducked beneath dialogue, intentional silence and sparing sound effects; edit by importing, selecting, arranging, trimming, adding transitions, titles, captions and sound, color correcting and exporting—team members do all editing.','Use mostly clean cuts with pacing that fits the tone; synchronize sound and video (clap to sync); titles, credits and captions count toward 30 seconds, but optional pure black leads do not; export in high quality and check on several devices.'],terms:[['Ducking','Lowering music volume beneath speech.'],['Synchronization','Sound and video happening together precisely.'],['Room tone','The natural background sound of a location, used to smooth edits.']],mcqs:[q('Where should the microphone be placed?','Close to the speaker','Across the room','Behind the camera','Outside','Clarity.'),q('What is ducking?','Lowering music under speech','A type of shot','A copyright form','Raising music volume','Audio.'),q('Which transition is most common professionally?','A simple cut','A star wipe','A page curl','A spinning cube','Editing.'),q('How long do most shots last in a 30-second PSA?','About 1–3 seconds','About 15 seconds','About 30 seconds','Less than 0.1 second','Pacing.'),q('Why clap at the start of a take?','To sync separately recorded audio','To scare actors','To start music','For luck','Sync.'),q('Do optional pure black leads count toward the time limit?','No','Yes','Only at the start','Only at the end','Rule.'),q('Who must do the editing?','Team members','A professional studio','An AI service','The judges','Rule.'),q('Why check the final mix on phone speakers?','Judges may watch that way','It is required by law','It improves video','It shortens the PSA','Testing.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would record audio and edit the PSA for high technical scores.',rubric:[['Recording',['quiet','microphone','close','lavalier','room tone','headphones']],['Balance',['music','beneath','ducking','silence','sound effects','clear']],['Editing',['timeline','trim','cuts','transitions','pacing','captions','color']],['Sync and finishing',['sync','clap','30 seconds','black','export','devices','team']]],model:'To earn high audio scores, we would record dialogue in quiet rooms away from fans and traffic, clip a lavalier microphone on each speaker or place a phone just out of frame, monitor with headphones, record room tone at each location and re-record any noisy lines; our narrator would record voice-over in a closet lined with clothes to reduce echo. In the mix, speech would always be clear, with upbeat royalty-free music ducked beneath narration and raised during visual-only moments, one intentional pause of silence before the call to action and a few quick sound effects, such as a cheerful “ding” when the brain battery charges. Our team would do all editing ourselves: import and organize footage, select the best takes, arrange clips on the timeline following the storyboard, trim each to one to three seconds for an energetic montage, use clean cuts and occasional match cuts and fades, add short titles, captions and credits, color correct for consistency and export in 1080p. For synchronization, a teammate would clap on camera at the start of each take so we could align separately recorded audio, and we would cut on music beats. We would check that the runtime from the first visual or sound to the last is 30 seconds or less, remembering that titles and credits count but optional pure black leads do not, and we would watch the final file on phone speakers, headphones and a laptop before uploading.'},sources:[RULES,COMM]};

export const PS8={title:'Music, copyright and credits',hook:'A catchy song can make a PSA memorable—and a copyright violation can make it ineligible to air or cost your team points. The guidelines require written permission for any copyright-protected music, logos or materials and a completed Copyright Form. Knowing the rules lets you use music and images confidently and credit sources properly.',objectives:['Explain copyright and why recorded music needs permission.','Find legal music and images for the PSA.','Handle logos, trademarks and incidental appearances.','Complete the Copyright Form and credit HFC correctly.'],sections:[
['Copyright basics',[
'**Copyright** protects original creative works—music, recordings, videos, images, text and characters—as soon as they are created. Using someone else’s work generally requires **permission** unless it is in the **public domain** or licensed for your use.',
'The guidelines state that the use of recorded music in a video is **not covered by fair use or educational exemptions** for this event. Teams and their advisor are responsible for obtaining releases and meeting legal requirements, and **written permission** is required for copyright-protected material.',
'When in doubt, create original content or use properly licensed material.',
"Copyright also applies to video clips, photos and artwork found online, even if they have no copyright notice. Assume material is protected unless you have clear evidence otherwise.",
"If you are unsure whether material is protected, contact your advisor or school media specialist before using it. Replacing a questionable song early is much easier than re-editing after the PSA is finished.",
sort('Needs permission or license?',['Needs permission or license','Can be used freely'],[['A popular song by a famous artist',0],['Music your team composed and recorded',1],['A movie clip',0],['Public domain image from a government agency',1],['A cartoon character from a TV show',0],['Your own original drawings',1]])]],
['Finding legal music',[
'**Royalty-free music** is usually stock music purchased for a single fee with no ongoing royalties. Your school media center, a TV production class or a local station may have royalty-free libraries. Some music is licensed under **Creative Commons** licenses that allow use with attribution—read the terms carefully.',
'The best option for originality is **original music**: a team member composes and records a simple melody or beat. Keep documentation of every music source for the Copyright Form.',
'“Free to listen” online does not mean free to use in a video.',
"Read license terms carefully. Some Creative Commons licenses forbid commercial use or modifications, and some require that your work be shared under the same license.",
match('Music source and requirement',[['Royalty-free library track','Keep the license or receipt'],['Creative Commons track','Follow terms and give attribution'],['Original team music','Document that the team created it'],['Popular commercial song','Written permission needed'],['School media library','Confirm the usage rights'],['Streaming service song','Not licensed for your video']])]],
['Logos, trademarks and incidental use',[
'**Trademarked** symbols, logos and characters require a **letter of permission** unless they appear only **incidentally**. Incidental means briefly visible in the background—such as a magazine in a crowd shot. If the camera **zooms in to emphasize** it, it is no longer incidental and permission is needed.',
'HOSA grants permission for chapters to use the **HOSA emblem and logo**. For **Hilarity for Charity** materials (logos, videos, graphics), you do **not need written permission**, but you **must credit HFC clearly**, such as in end credits or an on-screen acknowledgment.',
'Avoid prominent brand-name products, or cover or turn labels away from the camera.',
"Costumes and clothing can also create problems. Shirts with large brand logos or famous characters should be avoided or covered, especially in close-ups.",
"Logos on everyday items such as water bottles or laptops can be covered with tape or a sticker during filming, avoiding permission problems without changing the scene.",
sort('Permission needed?',['Permission needed','No permission needed'],[['A close-up of a soda brand logo',0],['A brand barely visible in a background crowd',1],['The HOSA emblem',1],['HFC logo with clear credit',1],['A famous cartoon character on a shirt in a close-up',0]])]],
['The Copyright Form',[
'The **Copyright Form** asks for the PSA title, school, team members’ last names and whether the video included any copyright-protected **music, logos, images, characters or symbols**. If yes, explain and **attach permission letters**, the royalty-free music source or other documentation.',
'The form is part of the combined PDF and is worth 5 points. Fill it out accurately and completely; a missing or incorrect form loses points and may raise questions about eligibility.',
"Keep a single shared folder for all permission documents, licenses and receipts. Gathering them as you go is much easier than searching for them right before the deadline.",
order('Completing the Copyright Form',['List every music, image and logo source used','Gather licenses, receipts and permission letters','Complete the form’s identifying information','Answer the copyright-protected materials question','Explain sources and attach documentation','Add it to the combined PDF'])]],
['Crediting sources and references',[
'Credit **HFC** clearly in the PSA if you use its materials and credit music or images as licenses require. The **reference page** in the PDF lists the literature and resources that guided the PSA in **APA** style—HFC resources, brain health organizations and any statistics you used.',
'Distinguish between organizations—HFC, the Alzheimer’s Association, the CDC and others each have their own missions and branding—and credit each accurately.',
"When crediting music or images in the PSA itself, keep credits short and readable. Fuller details can go on the reference page or the Copyright Form.",
ex('An APA reference','Write a reference for a web page (date invented for practice).',['Organization: Hilarity for Charity','Date: (2026) or n.d. if no date','Title in italics: 5 brain health habits','URL: https://www.wearehfc.org/5-brain-health-habits'],'Hilarity for Charity. (n.d.). *5 brain health habits*. https://www.wearehfc.org/5-brain-health-habits')]],
['Releases and privacy',[
'Get **signed releases** from everyone who appears on camera, and from parents or guardians for minors, following school policy. Get **location permission** to film at businesses, hospitals or other private property.',
'Do not include private health information or identifiable patients. Respect people’s wishes not to be filmed.',
"Include a short line in the credits such as “Actors appear with permission” if your school prefers it, and store the signed releases with your other documents.",
chk('What should you get from actors who appear in the PSA?',['Signed releases (and guardian consent for minors)','Nothing','Only their phone numbers','A copyright license'],'Permission to appear.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Copyright decisions',{a:{text:'Your team wants to use 15 seconds of a hit song. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Use royalty-free or original music instead, unless you obtain written permission','b','Correct.',true],['Use it—15 seconds is fair use','b','Recorded music is not covered by fair use here.',false]]},b:{text:'You include HFC’s logo at the end. What is required?',choices:[['Clear credit to Hilarity for Charity; written permission is not required','c','Correct.',true],['A signed letter from HFC','c','HFC materials need credit, not written permission.',false]]},c:{text:'Your Copyright Form is complete and accurate.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Copyright protects music, images, video and characters; recorded music is not covered by fair use or educational exemptions in this event; written permission is required for protected material.','Use royalty-free, properly licensed Creative Commons or original music and keep documentation; trademarked logos need permission unless incidental; HOSA emblem use is permitted; HFC materials need clear credit, not written permission.','Complete the Copyright Form accurately with attachments; list sources in APA on the reference page; distinguish organizations; get signed releases and location permissions.'],terms:[['Copyright','Legal protection for original creative works.'],['Royalty-free music','Music licensed for a one-time fee without ongoing royalties.'],['Incidental use','Brief, unemphasized appearance of protected material in the background.']],mcqs:[q('Is recorded music covered by fair use for this event?','No','Yes','Only under 10 seconds','Only for instrumental music','Rule.'),q('Which music option best supports originality?','Original team music','A hit song','A movie soundtrack','A streaming playlist','Original.'),q('Does using HFC’s logo require written permission?','No, but clear credit is required','Yes, always','It is prohibited','Only for videos','Sponsor rule.'),q('May HOSA chapters use the HOSA emblem?','Yes','No','Only with fees','Only in print','Permission granted.'),q('A brand logo zoomed in on screen:','Requires permission','Is incidental','Is always allowed','Is public domain','Emphasis.'),q('What should be attached to the Copyright Form if protected material is used?','Permission letters or license documentation','Nothing','A selfie','The script','Documentation.'),q('Which style is preferred for references?','APA','MLA only','None','Emoji','Health science.'),q('What should you get from minors appearing in the PSA?','Guardian-signed releases','Nothing','Their grades','A copyright license','Privacy.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would handle music, logos, images and credits legally and correctly in the PSA.',rubric:[['Copyright rules',['copyright','permission','written','recorded music','not fair use','public domain']],['Music and images',['royalty-free','creative commons','original','license','documentation']],['Logos and HFC',['trademark','incidental','hosa emblem','hfc','credit','no written permission']],['Forms and releases',['copyright form','attach','references','apa','releases','location']]],model:'Copyright protects music, recordings, images, video and characters, so we would assume anything we did not create needs permission unless it is in the public domain or licensed for our use. The PSA guidelines state that recorded music is not covered by fair use or educational exemptions, so rather than using a popular song, we would have a team member compose a simple upbeat track or use royalty-free music from our school media center, keeping the license or receipt; for any Creative Commons music or images, we would follow the license terms and give attribution. We would avoid prominent brand logos, turning labels away from the camera, because trademarked logos need written permission unless they appear only incidentally in the background, and zooming in would make them no longer incidental. HOSA allows chapters to use the HOSA emblem, and for Hilarity for Charity materials we would not need written permission but would credit HFC clearly in our end credits. We would complete the Copyright Form with our title, school and team names, answer whether we used any protected material and attach documentation for every source. On our APA reference page, we would list HFC’s brain health resources and other organizations such as alzheimers.gov and the CDC, crediting each accurately rather than mixing them up. Finally, we would collect signed releases from all actors, with guardian signatures for minors, and permission to film at each location.'},sources:[RULES,COPY,FAIR,CC]};

export const PS9={title:'Airing the PSA, measuring impact and uploading',hook:'A PSA that no one sees cannot change anything. The guidelines require your PSA to be shown to a real audience, documented with a signed Air Date Form, and measured with both numbers and stories. In Round Two, public use and public response are worth 15 points each—so airing and measuring impact are central to the event.',objectives:['Plan where and how to air the PSA for the target audience.','Complete the Air Date Form correctly.','Collect quantitative and qualitative impact data.','Assemble and upload the combined PDF and public link.'],sections:[
['Airing to a real audience',[
'The PSA must be shown to a **real audience**—at school, in the community, on local TV and/or online. Match the venue to your audience: school announcements, assemblies, classes and student social media for teens; community centers, libraries, senior centers, places of worship or local broadcasts for older adults; parent nights for parents.',
'Air it in **multiple places** to reach more people and gather more feedback. Ask partners such as school administrators, health departments or community organizations to help share it.',
'Plan airing early in spring so there is time to collect data.',
"Ask your school or community partners for permission before posting on their accounts or screens, and follow their policies for online content and comments.",
match('Audience and airing venue',[['High school students','Morning announcements and assemblies'],['Teens online','School-approved social media accounts'],['Older adults','Senior center screening'],['Parents','Parent-teacher night'],['Community','Library or community center display'],['Broad local audience','Local TV or radio station']])]],
['The Air Date Form',[
'The **Air Date Form** records the **PSA title**, **school**, **team members’ last names**, **link to the video**, **air date and time**, **location**, **estimated number of people reached** and comments, with the **signature, printed name and title** of an official—a school administrator or organization or station representative—confirming it was shown. Duplicate the form for additional air dates.',
'The form is worth 5 points in Round One. Fill it out completely and legibly before adding it to the PDF.',
"Estimate reach honestly. For an assembly, count attendance or use the school’s figures; for announcements, use the number of students and staff in the building.",
order('Completing the Air Date Form',['Schedule the airing with a school or community leader','Air the PSA','Record date, time, location and estimated reach','Have the official sign with printed name and title','Repeat for each airing','Add all forms to the combined PDF'])]],
['Measuring impact',[
'Collect **quantitative** data (numbers) and **qualitative** data (words and stories). Quantitative examples: **views, likes, comments and shares**; **audience size** at live screenings; **survey ratings** (such as the percentage who plan to try a pillar). Qualitative examples: **quotes**, **testimonials**, **comments** and **reactions**.',
'Also reflect on **who** saw the PSA using simple demographics—age, affiliation (students, parents, staff, patients) and, if relevant, cultural or other identity—to judge whether you reached your intended audience.',
'Even simple surveys or comment cards provide valuable evidence.',
"Collect data soon after viewers see the PSA, while it is still fresh in their minds. A follow-up question a week later can show whether anyone actually tried a pillar.",
"Combine numbers and stories in your reflection. A percentage shows scale, while a single quote shows how one real person responded to the message.",
sort('Quantitative or qualitative?',['Quantitative','Qualitative'],[['540 views on the school account',0],['A student saying, “I’m going to bed earlier now”',1],['78% of survey respondents named a pillar',0],['A teacher’s comment about class discussion',1],['Audience of 350 at an assembly',0],['Social media comments about favorite scenes',1]])]],
['Designing a short survey',[
'A short survey after a screening might ask: What is one pillar of brain health you remember? How likely are you to try one this week (1–5)? What did you like about the PSA? Who are you (student, parent, staff)? Keep it brief, anonymous and easy to complete on paper or a QR-code form.',
'Analyze results with simple percentages and pull out strong quotes for your presentation.',
"Protect privacy in surveys. Do not ask for names or other identifying information, and follow school policies for collecting information from students.",
ex('Analyzing survey results','120 students completed a survey after an assembly (results invented for practice).',['96 named at least one pillar → 96 ÷ 120 = 80%','84 rated their likelihood to try a pillar as 4 or 5 → 70%','Most-remembered pillar: sleep','Strong quote: “The brain battery made me think about my sleep.”'],'80% recalled a pillar and 70% planned to act (results invented for practice).')]],
['Assembling the upload',[
'One member uploads **one combined PDF** containing the **reference page(s)**, **Copyright Form**, **Air Date Form(s)** and **every team member’s Brain Health 101 and 201 certificates**, plus a **separate hyperlink** to the PSA. The link must be **public**—judges will not request access, and links that do not open are not scored.',
'Test the link in a private or logged-out browser before uploading. Upload by **May 15 at midnight EST**; teams that do not upload cannot compete in Round Two. If your state uses the HOSA upload system, upload again for ILC.',
"Assemble the PDF in the order judges will check items—references, Copyright Form, Air Date Form and certificates—and label each section clearly.",
sort('Ready to upload?',['Ready','Not ready'],[['Link opens in a logged-out browser',0],['Link set to private',1],['All members’ 101 and 201 certificates in the PDF',0],['Forms uploaded as separate image files',1],['Air Date Form signed by a school official',0]])]],
['Reflecting on impact',[
'Use your data to reflect: Did you reach your intended audience? What did viewers take away? What would you change next time? This reflection becomes the **public use** and **public response** sections of your Round Two presentation.',
'Be honest about limitations—a small survey or one airing location—and explain how you would expand reach in the future.',
"Keep a simple impact log that lists every airing, its estimated reach, the data collected and notable comments. The log makes the Round Two presentation easy to prepare.",
chk('Which reflection best supports the Round Two public response item?',['“80% of surveyed students recalled a pillar, and many quotes mentioned sleep.”','“People liked it, probably.”','“We posted it once.”','“We didn’t check.”'],'Evidence-based reflection.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Impact planning',{a:{text:'Your PSA aired at an assembly, but no official signed the Air Date Form. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Ask the school administrator who approved the airing to sign, print their name and title','b','Correct.',true],['Sign it yourselves','b','An official must confirm the airing.',false]]},b:{text:'Your PSA link requires permission to view. What do you do?',choices:[['Set the link to public and test it logged out','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it; judges will request access','c','Judges will not request access.',false]]},c:{text:'Your upload is complete and your impact data are ready.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The PSA must air to a real audience (school, community, local TV and/or online); match venues to your audience and air in several places early in spring.','The Air Date Form records title, school, names, link, date, time, location, estimated reach and an official’s signature, name and title; collect quantitative (views, audience size, survey percentages) and qualitative (quotes, comments) data plus simple demographics.','Upload one PDF (references, Copyright Form, Air Date Form, all members’ 101 and 201 certificates) and a separate public link by May 15; test the link logged out; reflect honestly on reach and response for Round Two.'],terms:[['Quantitative data','Numerical information, such as views or survey percentages.'],['Qualitative data','Descriptive information, such as quotes and comments.'],['Reach','The estimated number of people who saw the PSA.']],mcqs:[q('Must the PSA be shown to a real audience?','Yes','No','Only online','Only to judges','Rule.'),q('Who must sign the Air Date Form?','A school or community official','A team member','A judge','No one','Verification.'),q('Which is quantitative data?','Number of views','A viewer’s quote','A teacher’s comment','A testimonial','Numbers.'),q('Which is qualitative data?','A viewer’s comment about the message','540 views','Audience of 350','78% recall','Words.'),q('The PSA link must be:','Public','Private','Password-protected','Emailed to judges','Access.'),q('What happens if a team does not upload by May 15?','It cannot compete in Round Two','It gets extra time','It presents anyway','Nothing','Rule.'),q('What demographic information is suggested?','Age and affiliation','Home addresses','Phone numbers','Grades','Reach.'),q('If 90 of 150 respondents recall a pillar, what percentage is that?','60%','90%','40%','15%','90 ÷ 150.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would air the PSA, document it and measure its impact, and what your upload must include.',rubric:[['Airing',['real audience','school','community','online','venues','audience']],['Air Date Form',['date','time','location','reach','signature','official']],['Impact data',['quantitative','qualitative','views','survey','quotes','demographics']],['Upload',['pdf','references','copyright','certificates','public link','may 15']]],model:'Because our target audience is high school students, we would air our PSA during morning announcements, at a spring assembly, in health science classes and on our school’s approved social media accounts, and we would also share it at a parent night and our local library to reach families. For each airing, we would complete an Air Date Form with our PSA title, school, team members’ last names, video link, date, time, location and estimated number of people reached, and have the administrator or organization representative sign it with their printed name and title. To measure impact, we would collect quantitative data such as social media views, likes and shares, assembly attendance and results from a short anonymous survey asking students to name a pillar, rate how likely they are to try one this week and identify whether they are students, parents or staff. We would also collect qualitative data—quotes, comments and reactions, such as a student saying the brain battery made them think about sleep. We would analyze the survey with simple percentages and reflect on whether we reached our intended audience and what we would change. For the upload, one member would submit one combined PDF with our APA reference page, Copyright Form, all Air Date Forms and every member’s Brain Health 101 and 201 certificates, plus a separate public link to the video that we tested in a logged-out browser, well before May 15 at midnight EST.'},sources:[RULES,EVAL,UPLOAD]};

export const PS10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'Your PSA plays. Judges watch. Then your team has five minutes to explain how it came to life, how the public used it and responded and what it means. Round Two is worth 85 points—60 of them for content—so a clear, evidence-filled story of your creative process can make the difference.',objectives:['Prepare the device and setup for showing the PSA.','Cover every Round Two content item.','Structure a five-minute presentation shared by all members.','Deliver with strong voice, presence and teamwork.'],sections:[
['Setup and rules',[
'Bring your own **battery-powered device** (laptop, tablet or portable player) with the PSA **loaded locally**—HOSA provides **no TV, power, Wi-Fi, screen or cables**. The PSA must be clearly visible to judges sitting up to **5 feet** away. The presentation should **begin with the PSA video** (5 points).',
'After the PSA, the team has **five minutes**, with a time card at one minute remaining. Notecards are allowed; electronic notecards must be on a **different device** than the PSA and not shown to judges. **Only the PSA** may be shown—**no props or costumes**. All members must take an active role.',
"Practice starting the PSA from the moment you enter the room. Know which file to open, set the volume in advance and decide who will press play.",
sort('Allowed in Round Two?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['A charged laptop with the PSA saved locally',0],['Streaming the PSA over venue Wi-Fi',1],['Index card notes',0],['Wearing the costume from the PSA',1],['Showing a slideshow of survey charts',1],['Electronic notecards on a separate device, not shown',0]])]],
['Content items',[
'Judges score four content items at **15 points each**: **creative process**—an exceptional description of how the team developed the PSA; **public use of the PSA**—a thoughtful plan and implementation for sharing it; **public response**—how the PSA will change or has changed the audience’s attitudes or behavior, supported by evidence; and **understanding of the subject, theme and purpose**—clear knowledge of brain health and the PSA’s goal.',
'Plan specific content for each item so none is overlooked.',
"Use the rating sheet to check your script: each content item should have its own clear section, so judges can easily find and score it.",
"Understanding of the subject can also include what your team learned in the Brain Health 101 and 201 courses and how that knowledge shaped which pillars you featured.",
match('Content item and what to include',[['Creative process','Concept, script, storyboard, filming and editing decisions'],['Public use','Where and how you aired it for your audience'],['Public response','Views, survey results and quotes'],['Understanding','Why brain health matters and the pillars featured'],['Began with PSA','Play the video first'],['Delivery','Voice, presence, diction, teamwork']])]],
['Telling the creative process story',[
'Explain **why** you made each major choice: why you chose your audience, genre and tone; how you developed the concept and tested the script; how the storyboard guided filming; technical decisions about lighting, audio and editing; and what problems you solved. Mention how you used (or did not use) AI responsibly and how you handled copyright.',
'Specific stories—“We reshot the bedtime scene three times because the lamp created harsh shadows”—show judges a thoughtful process.',
"Share one or two decisions you would change if you made the PSA again. Thoughtful reflection shows judges that your team learned from the process.",
order('Creative process story',['Research and Brain Health courses','Audience and message decisions','Concept brainstorming and testing','Script and storyboard','Filming choices and challenges','Editing, music and copyright'])]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A sample plan: **Opening (about 30 seconds)**—introduce the team and PSA goal. **Understanding (about 45 seconds)**—why brain health matters and the pillars featured. **Creative process (about 1½ minutes)**. **Public use (about 1 minute)**. **Public response (about 1 minute)**. **Close (about 15 seconds)**.',
'Divide sections so each member speaks equally, with smooth handoffs. Rehearse to about **4 minutes 45 seconds**.',
"Because no visuals besides the PSA are allowed, describe charts in words: “Four out of five students we surveyed remembered at least one pillar.”",
"Avoid spending too long on the creative process, which teams often enjoy describing. Use a timer in rehearsals to keep each section to its planned length.",
ex('Timing plan for four members','Split a five-minute presentation (one possible plan).',['Member 1: introduction and understanding of brain health (1:15)','Member 2: creative process—concept, script, storyboard (1:00)','Member 3: creative process—filming and editing; public use (1:15)','Member 4: public response and closing (1:15)','Total: 4:45'],'Every member speaks equally with a 15-second margin.')]],
['Presenting evidence of response',[
'Use specific numbers and quotes: “Our PSA reached about 1,200 people across five airings. In our survey of 120 students, 80% named at least one pillar, and 70% said they planned to try one this week. One student told us, ‘I’m going to stop scrolling at midnight.’”',
'Explain what the data mean and be honest about limitations. Describe how the PSA could continue to make an impact—future airings, partnerships or a youth brain health club.',
"Match your presentation energy to your PSA. If your video is upbeat and humorous, a warm, enthusiastic delivery reinforces the same message.",
"End with a short, memorable line that connects back to the PSA’s tagline. A strong close helps judges remember your team after watching many presentations.",
chk('Which statement best supports public response?',['“80% of 120 surveyed students named a pillar, and one said she now goes to bed earlier.”','“It went viral, we think.”','“People probably liked it.”','“We showed it once.”'],'Specific evidence.')]],
['Delivery',[
'Delivery items—**voice**, **stage presence**, **diction** and **team participation**—are worth 5 points each. Speak clearly and with energy that matches your PSA’s tone, make eye contact with each judge, avoid fillers and pronounce terms such as “cognitive” and “Alzheimer’s” correctly.',
'For team participation, every member should carry an **equal part**. Stand so the device remains visible, and practice starting the PSA smoothly—know exactly where the play button is and test the volume.',
"Practice handling questions or interruptions calmly. If judges ask something at the end, answer briefly and honestly, connecting your answer to your creative process or impact data.",
sort('Helps or hurts delivery?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Testing volume and playback before judges arrive',0],['Fumbling to find the video file',1],['Equal speaking parts',0],['One member presenting everything',1],['Eye contact with each judge',0],['Reading notecards word for word',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation day',{a:{text:'Your laptop battery is at 5% when you enter the room. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you have done?',choices:[['Charged it fully and brought a backup device with the PSA saved locally','b','Correct.',true],['Planned to plug in at the venue','b','No power is provided.',false]]},b:{text:'With one minute left, you have not discussed public response. What do you do?',choices:[['Share your strongest statistic and quote, then close','c','Correct.',true],['Keep describing editing','c','Public response is worth 15 points.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish on time with every content item covered.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Bring a charged, battery-powered device with the PSA saved locally (no power, Wi-Fi or screens provided), visible from 5 feet; begin with the PSA (5 points); then present for 5 minutes with no props, costumes or other visuals.','Content items at 15 points each: creative process, public use, public response and understanding of the subject, theme and purpose; tell the creative story with reasons and challenges and support response with numbers and quotes.','Plan about 4:45 with equal parts and smooth handoffs; deliver with clear voice, eye contact and correct pronunciation; test playback and volume beforehand and bring a backup device.'],terms:[['Public use','How and where the PSA was shared with its audience.'],['Public response','Evidence of how the audience reacted or changed.'],['Creative process','The steps and decisions used to develop the PSA.']],mcqs:[q('How should the presentation begin?','With the PSA video','With a slideshow','With costumes','With a quiz','Rubric item.'),q('How long is the presentation after the PSA?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','1 minute','Time.'),q('How far away must judges be able to see the PSA clearly?','Up to 5 feet','Up to 50 feet','Up to 1 inch','Any distance','Visibility.'),q('Which is provided by HOSA?','Nothing—teams bring their own device','A TV and cables','Wi-Fi','Power outlets','Rule.'),q('How many points is each main content item worth?','15','5','10','20','Rubric.'),q('Are props or costumes allowed?','No','Yes','Only costumes','Only props','Rule.'),q('Electronic notecards must be:','On a different device than the PSA and not shown','On the same device as the PSA','Shown to judges','Projected','Rule.'),q('Which supports public response best?','Specific survey results and quotes','A guess','Nothing','A slogan','Evidence.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline a five-minute Round Two presentation for your PSA that covers every scored element.',rubric:[['Setup',['battery','device','locally','5 feet','begin with the psa','no props']],['Content',['creative process','public use','public response','understanding','pillars']],['Evidence',['views','survey','percent','quotes','reach','limitations']],['Structure and delivery',['5 minutes','equal','handoffs','eye contact','4:45','backup']]],model:'We would arrive with a fully charged laptop and a backup tablet, both with the PSA saved locally, since HOSA provides no power, Wi-Fi or screens, and we would test the volume and position the screen so judges up to five feet away can see it clearly. We would begin by playing the PSA. Member 1 would introduce our team and explain our understanding of the topic: why brain health matters for memory, mood and learning, that dementia is not a normal part of aging, and the four pillars we featured—sleep, nutrition, stress management and social and physical activity. Member 2 would describe our creative process from completing the Brain Health 101 and 201 courses to choosing high school students as our audience, brainstorming the “brain battery” concept, testing the storyboard with classmates and writing our own 58-word script. Member 3 would explain filming and editing choices, such as reshooting the bedtime scene to fix harsh shadows, composing original music to avoid copyright problems and crediting Hilarity for Charity, and then describe public use: morning announcements, an assembly, health classes, our school’s social media and a parent night, documented on signed Air Date Forms. Member 4 would present public response: about 1,200 people reached, 80% of 120 surveyed students naming a pillar, 70% planning to try one and a student’s quote about going to bed earlier, plus honest limitations and next steps, before closing. We would rehearse to about 4 minutes 45 seconds, use notecards only and give each member an equal part.'},sources:[RULES,HFC]};
